🔹 Execution Order in Java (High Level)

When a class is used, Java follows this order:

1️⃣ Static variables
2️⃣ Static blocks
⬇
3️⃣ main() method
⬇
4️⃣ Object creation (new)

Instance variables

Instance blocks

Constructor

⚠️ Static methods execute only when explicitly called

🧩 Example Program (Must-Remember)
class Test {

    static {
        System.out.println("1. Static Block");
    }

    Test() {
        System.out.println("4. Constructor");
    }

    static void staticMethod() {
        System.out.println("3. Static Method");
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("2. Main Method");

        staticMethod();

        Test t = new Test();
    }
}

▶️ Step-by-Step Execution
Step 1️⃣ Class Loading

JVM loads class Test

Executes static block

1. Static Block

Step 2️⃣ main() Starts
2. Main Method

Step 3️⃣ Static Method Call

Executes only when called

3. Static Method

Step 4️⃣ Object Creation

Constructor executes

4. Constructor

🖨️ Final Output
1. Static Block
2. Main Method
3. Static Method
4. Constructor

🔍 Important Rules (Interview Gold)
✔ Static Block

Executes once

Executes when class is loaded

Used for static initialization

✔ Constructor

Executes every time object is created

Used to initialize instance variables

✔ Static Method

Does not execute automatically

Must be called explicitly

Can access only static members

❌ Common Interview Traps
❓ Will constructor execute before static block?

No ❌
Static block always executes first.

❓ Will static method run automatically?

No ❌
Only runs when called.

❓ How many times static block runs?

Once per class loading

🧠 Memory View (Quick)

Static block → Method Area

Constructor → Heap (object creation)

Static method → Method Area

🟢 One-Line Interview Answer

Static blocks execute first during class loading, constructors execute on object creation, and static methods execute only when explicitly called.